  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA strong low level jet is pivoting eastward across northern \nIllinois and Wisconsin early this morning. The bulk of the \nfaster flow remains within and above the inversion layer which \nwill continue to hold most gusts at bay until after daybreak. \nThereafter, diurnal mixing will drive a quick increase in \nmomentum transfer after about 9 AM resulting in the development \nof some 20-25 mph surface gusts. While the jet will weaken and \nlift north through the day, slightly deeper mixing will likely \nkeep at least occasional 25 to perhaps a few 30 mph gusts going\nthrough the afternoon, primarily across northern Illinois. \nOtherwise, it's looking like another wonderful day with \ntemperatures expected to push well into the 60s with some \noccasional passing high cloud cover. \n\nTonight, some moisture return will occur, particularly just off\nthe surface directed across northern Illinois and southern\nWisconsin. As winds ease, this may promote a potential for\nsome shallow patchy fog development. There's some spread \nregarding the wind response around 950 mb across the guidance\nsuite, and the potential for dewpoints to mix out a bit this\nafternoon also casts some uncertainty around the fog potential.\nNonetheless, have introduced a small patchy fog area north of\nI-88 with this latest forecast. \n\nA broad longwave trough will begin to cut east of the\nContinental Divide on Friday, with an attendant upper jet streak\npushing across the Northern Plains. This will induce the next\nround of surface cyclogenesis to our west. We'll pick up some\nmodest southerly breezes on Friday afternoon, and if cloud cover\ndoesn't get too opaque, a few sites may make a run at 70\ndegrees. This will put the record highs for the day (68 at\nO'Hare and 69 at Rockford) within reach, although the latest\nforecast remains just shy of these values. Saturday looks\nsimilarly warm. The latest guidance continues to slow the \neastward progression of the incoming front and we'll\ncommensurately show precip-free conditions continuing into the\nevening. \n\nShower chances will increase overnight Saturday into Sunday\nmorning with the cold front shifting east across the area during\nthe afternoon. Breezy northerly winds in the wake of the front\nwill usher in a period of seasonably cooler conditions into the\nmiddle of next week. \n\nCarlaw",
